  "body": "Writing a base value back over a draft that was already modified discards the draft, and the produced state correctly keeps the original value. The patches, however, still contain the discarded draft's changes:\n\n```js\nenablePatches()\n\nconst child = {id: 1}\nconst base = {child}\n\nconst [next, patches] = produceWithPatches(base, draft => {\n\tdraft.child.id = 2\n\tdraft.child = child // put the base value back\n})\n\nnext // {child: {id: 1}}\npatches // [{op: \"replace\", path: [\"child\", \"id\"], value: 2}]\napplyPatches(base, patches) // {child: {id: 2}}\n```\n\nThe same happens for array elements, Map entries, and drafts nested inside the discarded one. Anything that replays the patches (an undo stack, a server applying client patches, a second copy of the state) ends up with a value the producer never kept. This is a regression from the finalization rewrite in #1183; before it, the patches above were empty.\n\n`getPath` checks whether the value at a draft's key still belongs to that draft, and accepted the draft, its copy, or its base. A draft is stored in its parent's copy as soon as it is created, so the base can only be back at that key when the original value was written back, which is exactly when the draft has been discarded. Accepting only the draft or its copy skips patch generation for discarded drafts, including the drafts nested inside them.\n\nThe regression tests cover objects, nested drafts, arrays and Maps. Before the fix, 8 of the 16 cases fail; afterwards all of them pass.\n\nValidation (Node 26.5.0, Yarn 1.22.22, Windows):\n- `yarn test`: 4,212 source tests and 3,661 production-build tests passed, with 8 existing skips in each suite; build and Flow passed.\n- `yarn test:perf`, Prettier for both changed files, and `git diff --check` passed.\n- A randomized round-trip check (random mutations on nested objects, arrays, Maps and Sets, verifying that the patches reproduce the result and the inverse patches restore the base) failed on this pattern before the fix and passes 60,000 runs afterwards.\n",
